#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163117} :activity/id #uuid "a75a57eb-1a2d-59cc-9314-4e4f1453c437" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048327 :topic/id #uuid "9576099a-1e2b-5b2b-bca5-c78638a7b4b8" :topic/math-academy-id 3137} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163344 :tutorial/content "Suppose that a company is about to elect a new union representative. We're interested in determining the proportion $p$ of a company's employees that will vote for \"candidate $A$.\"\n\nIf the company has $N = 50$ employees, and $30$ of them will vote for candidate $A$, then the proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\np = \\dfrac{30}{50} = 0.6\n$$\n\nIn other words, $60\\%$ of the company's employees will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $N$ is the **population size**\n- $p$ is the **population proportion**\n- The number of employees that will vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n$Np = 50\\cdot 0.6 = 30$.\n\nIn this lesson, our goal is to build foundations for estimating $p$ in cases where the population size $N$ is very large. Note that although the population size $N$ is not very large in this example, we can still use it to help develop the key ideas.\n\nWe start by modeling the response from each member of the population as a random variable $X_i$, defined by\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population will vote for candidateA; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,50\n$$\n\nCalculating the probability that a randomly selected member of the population will vote for candidate $A$ is straightforward:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X_{i} = 1) &= \\frac{the number of members that will vote for candidateA}{population size} \\\\\n&= \\frac{Np}{N} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30}{50} \\\\\n&= 0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** It's important to note that the $X_i$'s are *dependent!* For example,\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 1) \\neq P(X_2 = 1)\n$$\n\nTo see why, note that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1) = 0.6\n$$\n\nas before. However,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X_{2} = 1 \\mid X_{1} = 1) &= ((the number of members that will vote for candidateA) - 1)/(population size - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{Np - 1}{N - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{50 \\cdot 0.6 - 1}{50 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{30 - 1}{49} \\\\\n&= \\frac{29}{49} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.592 \\\\\n&\\ne P(X_{2} = 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's interpret this result. We know that the first member will vote for candidate $A$. So, to compute the conditional probability that the second member will *also* vote for candidate $A$, we consider a reduced population with $N-1 = 49$ members, of which only $30-1 = 29$ will vote for candidate $A$.\n\nCalculating\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 0)\n$$\n\nworks similarly, as we'll see in the following example." :tutorial/id #uuid "3fb9dab1-fabb-573d-8f7b-d06c37474163" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6461 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b7e31dee-05b0-5626-92d4-2ad066905c81" :step/math-academy-id 39024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163119}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162137 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161703 :question/id #uuid "1ad81fed-5e71-5df6-abc4-a09cd5c432fc" :question/math-academy-id "e-10362"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c6e806b-288e-5ff8-b54d-4b19c16cd2f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050606}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162439}] :knowledge-point/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "3959ad96-797b-5cb6-937c-25ee4bd8fbf5" :step/math-academy-id 39025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163119 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162138 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161704 :question/id #uuid "b162ec77-b73f-50f7-b2d2-b6c82bdb7d67" :question/math-academy-id "e-10363"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b9274c87-2d68-5fca-af3e-5c47b9541bac" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048456}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162434}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Conditional Probability Given a Population Proportion"} :step/id #uuid "1a5db991-df51-5d92-b1cc-245ea8960755" :step/math-academy-id 39030 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163125}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162139 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161705 :question/id #uuid "a623014c-203f-5c40-87b5-e847782a8ea1" :question/math-academy-id "e-10364"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7e5587a-5a45-59b6-ae59-df68ed33e0fb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050425} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051082}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162433}] :knowledge-point/title "Properties of Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "0c4a9d24-fd2f-52ed-94cd-513aa0657bab" :step/math-academy-id 39031 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163126}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163124 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163345 :tutorial/content "Now, suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables *provided that* the population size $N$ is *significantly larger* than the sample size $n$ (which we denote as $N \\gg n$). In practice, this typically means that the sample size should be no larger than $5\\%$ of the population size.\n\nThe assumption that the population size is much larger than the sample size is true in many cases. For example, the U.S. voting population consists of hundreds of millions of people, yet a typical sample from this population might consist of only a few thousand voters.\n\nLet's now consider the following statistic:\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i\n$$\n\nwhere $X_i \\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$.\n\nSince the $X_i$'s take the value $1$ if the $i$th element has the characteristic or $0$ if the $i$th element doesn't have the characteristic, the statistic $X$ can be used to count the number of elements with the characteristic. In other words, $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic.\n\nWe wish to determine the sampling distribution of $X$. Note the following:\n\n- Each $X_i$ can be modeled as a Bernoulli random variable, i.e., $X_i\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$.\n- Since we're assuming that $N\\gg n$, it follows that $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ are (approximately) *independent and identically distributed* (I.I.D) Bernoulli random variables.\n- It can be shown that a sum of $n$ I.I.D. Bernoulli random variables with parameter $p$ is a binomial random variable $B(n,p)$ (we'll prove this in a separate lesson). Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nWe summarize this process using the following flow chart.\n\n![](images/f5/f50d54432c86d4b41ed842218a70b93617457de3e518aa88cbe6ea28762f37ea.png)\n\nFinally, using the formulas for the mean and variance of a Binomial random variable, we have the following approximations:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "77f66879-2dda-57f9-b96c-2ad1166282c8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6477 :tutorial/title "The Number of Sample Elements With a Characteristic"} :step/id #uuid "7f0864d1-c890-56d0-ad56-9af735a0fb71" :step/math-academy-id 39212 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163120}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163125 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163346 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have a population of size $N=1\\,000$ of which the proportion $p=0.5$ has a particular characteristic. We denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the population has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,1\\,000\n$$\n\nTechnically speaking, as we saw previously, the $X_i$'s are *dependent*.\n\nThis time, however, since the population size $N = 1\\,000$ is large, we may *approximate* that the $X_i$'s are *independent*.\n\nTo see why, we first note that\n\n$$\nP(X_2=1) = \\dfrac{Np}{N} = 0.5\n$$\n\nNow, computing\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 1)\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X_{2} = 1 \\mid X_{1} = 1) &= \\frac{Np - 1}{N - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1000 \\cdot 0.5 - 1}{1000 - 1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{500 - 1}{999} \\\\\n&= \\frac{499}{999} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we've shown that\n\n$$\nP(X_2 = 1 \\mid X_1 = 1) \\approx P(X_2 = 1)\n$$\n\nsuggesting that $X_1$ and $X_2$ are (approximately) independent." :tutorial/id #uuid "a129ef6b-606a-5b23-8f65-0573d4422fcd" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6479 :tutorial/title "Independence in Large Populations"} :step/id #uuid "16f70a0f-60a9-55ce-a435-a9dd8530d4d9" :step/math-academy-id 39214 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163124}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163126 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163347 :tutorial/content "Suppose we conduct a random sample of size $n$ where some proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let's denote the random variable $X_i$ as follows:\n\n${1, [math]i[/math]th member of the sample has the characteristic; 0, otherwise$\nfor\n\n$$\ni = 1,\\ldots,n\n$$\n\nWe know that if the population sample size $n$ is small compared to the population size $N$, then the $X_i$'s can be modeled as mutually independent random variables. Therefore,\n\n$$\nX = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i \\approx B(n,p)\n$$\n\nRemember that $X$ represents the number of sample elements with the characteristic. Therefore, we can form an *estimate* for the population proportion $p$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = \\dfrac{X}{n} = \\dfrac1 n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} X_i = \\overline{X}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}$. First, recall that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = np, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X] = np(1-p)\n$$\n\nTherefore, for the mean of $\\widehat{\\,p}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[p^(^)] &= E[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot E[X] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n} \\cdot np \\\\\n&= p\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the variance of $\\widehat{\\,p}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[p^(^)] &= Var[\\frac{X}{n}] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot Var[X] \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n^{2}} \\cdot np(1 - p) \\\\\n&= (p(1 - p))/(n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, we have the following important results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = p, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$\n\nIt should be noted that we don't yet know the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$. What we *do* know is that whatever the sampling distribution of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is, it has mean $p$ and variance\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "8ecb0abb-cc8a-58b3-b310-fabeeb9c4aca" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6480 :tutorial/title "Mean and Variance of Sample Proportions"} :step/id #uuid "656c4a52-6bb0-59f8-878d-58db462b17ee" :step/math-academy-id 39215 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163128}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163128 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163349 :tutorial/content "The standard deviation of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ is called the **standard error** of $\\widehat{\\,p}$ and is denoted $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\,p}]} = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{p(1-p)}{n}}\n$$\n\nIf $p$ is unknown, we can replace it with its estimate $\\widehat{\\,p}$ in the standard error formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}] = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n$$\n\nNote that we have written $\\widehat{\\textrm{SE}}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$ to denote that the above expression is an estimate of $\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,p}]$." :tutorial/id #uuid "34af16d9-40e0-5de6-9e2d-753be4cea679" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6484 :tutorial/title "The Standard Error"} :step/id #uuid "8e2f6423-528f-586d-8112-c73e1f771a8a" :step/math-academy-id 39234 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163118}}] :activity/title "Sampling Proportions From Finite Populations" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162231}]}
